e performance targets: at APC 0 the STARK excl trace is 2654ms (regressed from 2153ms baseline), and at APC 300 it's 1433ms (improved from 2455ms baseline). For 2.0x scaling I need APC 300 below 1327ms, which means finding another 106ms of improvement. With the healthier baseline of 2153ms at APC 0, I'd actually need APC 300 under 1077ms — a much steeper target. The most impactful direction appears to be further optimization in that area.\n\nLooking at where improvements could help APC 300 without hurting APC 0, I'm analyzing the Round 0 and LogUp GKR components. Round 0 shows 301ms at APC 300 versus 177ms at APC 0, with the difference coming from handling 623 small AIRs versus 99 AIRs — the multi-stream approach already helps here, but further gains would require reducing per-AIR overhead or improving SM utilization. LogUp GKR is interesting because it actually performs better at APC 300 (526ms) than APC 0 (1383ms), since the protocol complexity scales with trace height which is smaller for the many small AIRs at APC 300, though the input evaluation still scales with AIR count.\n\nComparing the performance across all components, I can see that LogUp GKR is already scaling well, but Round 0 and MLE Rounds are the real bottlenecks — they both get significantly worse with more AIRs, while Trace Commit and WHIR actually improve at APC 300 due to the cell reduction, and Stacked Reduction stays flat.\n\nRound 0 is taking up 21% of the STARK time at APC 300, and the issue is clear when I look at the per-AIR costs: at APC 300 it's only 0.48ms per AIR (301ms ÷ 623 AIRs), but at APC 0 it's 1.79ms per AIR (177ms ÷ 99 AIRs). This suggests the overhead is dominated by per-AIR costs rather than per-cell costs, which is why Round 0 gets worse even though there are fewer cells to process.\n\nThe real bottleneck is that Round 0 and the MLE rounds together consume 471ms—about a third of the total STARK time at APC 300. If I could cut those in half, I'd save 236ms and get the scaling