n\nLooking at what's left: Round 0 is still spending 301ms with per-AIR kernel launches and memory transfers that resist batching; LogUp GKR input eval is already optimized but could benefit from better result collection; and Trace Commit has two clear targets—the calculate_zero_hash function running 584 times is begging for batching, and the Poseidon2 hashing at 231ms across 25 traces might also be consolidatable.\n\nThe Merkle tree construction is smaller and less uniform, so probably not worth the effort. MLE rounds are already batched, and the openings are well-tuned. So calculate_zero_hash really stands out as the next win—though I need to verify it's actually in the STARK exclusion trace path and whether 109ms of savings would meaningfully move the needle on the overall 1-second budget.\n\nLet me step back and look at what the NVTX profiling actually shows about time distribution. The spec.py tool already parses those metrics, so I should dig into the per-segment breakdown to see if there are other hotspots I'm missing. Let me check the metrics JSON structure directly to understand the full picture.\n\nLooking at the APC 300 breakdown, the major components are Constraints at 999ms, Openings at 177ms, and Trace Commit at 255ms, which accounts for the full 1433ms. Within Constraints, LogUp GKR dominates at 526ms, followed by Round 0 at 301ms and MLE Rounds at 170ms. Now I need to dig deeper into what's happening inside LogUp GKR to find optimization opportunities.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5538,"cache_read_input_tokens":61467,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5538},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"794cedb4-74b2-4dd2-b512-5aabd1b44ebc","uuid":"25886dbd-f92b-49a2-bcfd-d69c8608cefd"}
