{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XgKPCodThv5YBMeSqPCj4F","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Dp5KkZAhmqRxX8kHPvjXQH","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Research GKR protocol code path","subagent_type":"Explore","prompt":"I'm analyzing GPU prover performance in a STARK backend. The LogUp GKR protocol (fractional_sumcheck) takes ~214ms at APC 300 and hasn't been optimized yet.\n\nI need to understand the GKR protocol code path in the cuda-backend:\n1. The `fractional_sumcheck_gpu` function — how it works, what it calls\n2. The key GKR kernels: `compute_round_and_revert_kernel`, `frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel`, `bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel`, `multifold_kernel`, `precompute_m_build_partial_kernel`\n3. How many rounds the protocol runs and what work is done per round\n4. Whether there are per-AIR or per-trace iterations within the protocol (as opposed to bulk GPU operations)\n5. Any synchronization points or sequential dependencies between rounds\n\nLook in:\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/` for Rust code\n- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/` for CUDA kernels\n- Search for `fractional_sumcheck`, `gkr_rounds`, `frac_build`, `multifold`\n\nThoroughness: medium. Report file paths and line numbers. I want to understand if there's per-AIR overhead that could be batched, or if the protocol is already operating on bulk data."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1950,"cache_read_input_tokens":86598,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1950},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"794cedb4-74b2-4dd2-b512-5aabd1b44ebc","uuid":"8b7aa985-70ef-43db-9fb5-6d79bce8fcfd"}
