{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ABYZ8DUsPR7cFWY3URMuTj","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t\n201\t**Advancing rounds.** Eq layers are precomputed upfront; each sumcheck round calls `drop_layer()` (no GPU eq-fold kernel). Precompute-M uses `eq_tail_ptrs` to select low/high pointers for the tail part after skipping window variables.\n202\t\n203\t### Sumcheck round strategies\n204\t\n205\tIn the code, the loop `for round in 1..total_rounds` handles GKR rounds `j = 2, ..., n` with `j = round + 1`. GKR round `j = 1` (trivial: 0-round sumcheck) is handled by the initialization above. At the start of code `round`, `xi_prev` holds `ξ^{(round)}` (= `ξ^{(j-1)}`), and `lambda` holds `λ_j` (= `λ_{round+1}`).\n206\t\n207\tThe first sumcheck round (round 0) of each GKR round is always handled by `do_sumcheck_round_and_revert`, which fuses the tree layer revert with computing `s'_0(1)` and `s'_0(2)`. Subsequent sumcheck rounds use one of two strategies:\n208\t\n209\t#### Fold-eval strategy\n210\t\n211\tProcesses one variable per sumcheck round. At the start of round `t`, the buffer holds `2 * pq_size` evaluations of `p̂_j` and `q̂_j` partially evaluated at the previous challenges `ρ_0, ..., ρ_{t-2}`, but **not** yet folded by the latest challenge `ρ_{t-1}`. A single fused kernel does two things simultaneously:\n212\t\n213\t1. **Fold** the buffer by challenge `ρ_{t-1}`: for each pair, compute `even + ρ_{t-1} * (odd - even)`, halving `pq_size`. This produces the partially-evaluated multilinear values at `(ρ_0, ..., ρ_{t-1}, ·)`.\n214\t2. **Compute** `s'_t(1)` and `s'_t(2)` from the freshly folded values, multiplying by `eq` weights from `eq_buffer` and reducing via block sums.\n215\t\n216\t**Buffer strategy:** The `layer` buffer (size `2^n`) holds the tree and is needed for reverts in non-last GKR rounds. In those rounds, data moves from `layer` to `work_buffer` on the first fused fold, then continues in-place on `work_buffer` to preserve `layer`. On the **last GKR round** (`j = n`), no future reverts are needed, so scheduling is flexible: the implementation may use ping-pong between `layer` and `work_buffer` when capacity permits (to use non-aliasing kernels), or stay in-place otherwise. The final fold after the last sumcheck round is standalone (no next round to compute) and runs on whichever buffer currently holds active data.\n217\t\n218\tKernels:\n219\t- `compute_round_and_revert_kernel` — fused revert + compute (sumcheck round 0 only)\n220\t- `compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel` — fused compute + fold (in-place)\n221\t- `compute_round_and_fold_kernel` — fused compute + fold (out-of-place, ping-pong)\n222\t- `fold_ef_columns_kernel` — standalone fold (final inner round)\n223\t- `static_final_reduce_block_sums` — reduce per-block partial sums to `s'(1)` and `s'(2)`\n224\t\n225\t#### Precompute-M strategy\n226\t\n227\tProcesses `w` variables at once (`w = GKR_WINDOW_SIZE = 3`). It is not work-efficient: M-build is `O(2^{n+w})` vs `O(w * 2^n)` for `w` fold-eval rounds (a `2^w / w` arithmetic overhead), but it reduces global-memory passes by evaluating `w` rounds from a small `4^w` matrix.\n228\t\n229\tWe follow ideas in [Section 4, https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/1473] as applied to sumcheck on the GKR layer polynomial.\n230\t\n231\t**Derivation.** The GKR round polynomial has the form (see Part 1):\n232\t\n233\t```\n234\ts_i(X) = eq((r, X), z_r) * sum_{u,v in {0,1}^{i-1}} eq(u, r) * eq(v, r)\n235\t          * [sum_{b in {0,1}^{n-i-1}} (p_0(u,X,b)*q_1(v,X,b) + p_1(u,X,b)*q_0(v,X,b)\n236\t              + lambda * q_0(u,X,b)*q_1(v,X,b)) * eq(b, z_b)]\n237\t```\n238\t\n239\twhere `p_0, q_0` and `p_1, q_1` are the two halves (first variable = 0 or 1) and `z_r, z_b` are the corresponding parts of `z = xi^{(j-1)}`. The `eq((r,X), z_r)` factor is separated because it only depends on `r` and `X`, not on `u, v, b`.\n240\t\n241\tWe precompute:\n242\t\n243\t```\n244\tM_{αβ}[u, v] = sum_{b in {0,1}^{n-i-1}} (p_0(u,α,b)*q_1(v,β,b) + p_1(u,α,b)*q_0(v,β,b)\n245\t                + lambda * q_0(u,α,b)*q_1(v,β,b)) * eq(b, z_b)\n246\t```\n247\t\n248\tfor `α, β in {0, 1}`. Then `s'_i(X)` (with the `eq((r,X), z_r)` factor removed) is a degree-2 polynomial in `X` expressible in terms of the four `M_{αβ}` tables via multilinearity:\n249\t\n250\t```\n251\ts'_i(X) = sum_{u,v} eq(u,r) * eq(v,r) * [(1-X)^2 * M_{00}[u,v] + (1-X)*X * (M_{01}[u,v] + M_{10}[u,v]) + X^2 * M_{11}[u,v]]\n252\t```\n253\t\n254\t**Windowed implementation.** In the code, `w = GKR_WINDOW_SIZE` sumcheck rounds are handled per window. The M matrix is `2^w x 2^w`, where the row index `u` and column index `v` are each `w`-bit numbers corresponding to the window variables.\n255\t\n256\tAt sumcheck round `t` within the window (`t = 0, ..., w - 1`), each `w`-bit index is decomposed as `[prefix (t bits)] [current X (1 bit)] [suffix (w - t - 1 bits)]`. The prefix bits correspond to challenges already sampled in this window; the suffix bits correspond to window positions not yet reached. The round polynomial evaluation becomes:\n257\t\n258\t```\n259\ts'_t(X) = sum_{b1,b2 in {0,1}^t} sum_{s in {0,1}^{w-t-1}}\n260\t          eq_r_prefix[b1] * eq_r_prefix[b2] * eq_suffix[s] * f(X, M[b1|X|s, b2|X|s])\n261\t```\n262\t\n263\twhere:\n264\t- `eq_r_prefix` = MLE table of the `t` challenges sampled so far in this window, i.e. `eq_r_prefix[b] = eq(b, (r_0, ..., r_{t-1}))`\n265\t- `eq_suffix` = MLE table of the remaining `xi_prev` values for suffix positions, i.e. `eq_suffix[s] = eq(s, (z_{t+1}, ..., z_{w-1}))`\n266\t- `f(X, ...)` combines the four `M_{αβ}` entries using multilinearity in `X` as above\n267\t\n268\tNote that `u` and `v` share the same suffix bits `s` — this is because both sides of the product (`p_0 * q_1`, etc.) share the same hypercube variables `b` in the M definition, so the suffix contraction is shared.\n269\t\n270\tAt round `t = 0`: `eq_r_prefix` is trivial (size 1), `eq_suffix` has size `2^{w-1}`. At round `t = w - 1`: `eq_r_prefix` has size `2^{w-1}`, `eq_suffix` is trivial (size 1). The total work per round is `O(4^w)` — scanning the M matrix — regardless of the evaluation buffer size.\n271\t\n272\tThe M build can start from two different states, corresponding to two kernel variants (`precompute_m_build_partial_kernel<inline_fold>`):\n273\t\n274\t- **After a multifold** (or at the start of a subsequent window): the evaluation buffer is already folded and has `2^{rem_n}` elements. The kernel reads directly from the buffer (`inline_fold = false`).\n275\t- **After a compute-and-revert round** (first window of each GKR round): the buffer still has `2^{rem_n+1}` elements because the fold by the previous challenge `r_prev` has not yet been applied. The kernel folds inline while building M (`inline_fold = true`), computing `val = lo + r_prev * (hi - lo)` on the fly.\n276\t\n277\tSteps for each window of `w` rounds:\n278\t\n279\t1. **Build M** (`frac_precompute_m_build_raw`) — For each tail point `b`, compute the contributions to `M[u,v]` weighted by `eq(b, z_b)` and `lambda`, and accumulate. Parallelized over tail points: each CUDA block processes a tile of `tail_tile` tail points, producing a partial M matrix. Partials are reduced by `precompute_m_reduce_partials_kernel`.\n280\t\n281\t2. **Eval rounds** (repeat `w` times) — For each round `t`, compute `s'_t(1)` and `s'_t(2)` from M by contracting with `eq_r_prefix` and `eq_suffix` as described above. This is a small kernel (`precompute_m_eval_round_kernel`) operating on the `4^w`-sized M matrix. Each round calls `observe_and_update` to sample `r_t` and update accumulators. No buffer folding happens during these rounds.\n282\t\n283\t3. **Multifold** (`frac_multifold_raw`) — After the window, fold the evaluation buffer by all deferred challenges at once. When `pending_fold` is true (first window), this includes the pending `r_prev` plus the `w` window challenges, folding `w + 1` coordinates total (`pq_size >>= w + 1`). Otherwise it folds only the `w` window challenges (`pq_size >>= w`). Computes `eq_r_window` = MLE table of all deferred challenges.\n284\t\n285\tAfter all windows, any remaining rounds fall back to the fold-eval path.\n286\t\n287\tKernels:\n288\t- `precompute_m_build_partial_kernel<false>` — build partial M blocks from an already-folded buffer\n289\t- `precompute_m_build_partial_kernel<true>` — same, but folds inline by `r_prev` (first window)\n290\t- `precompute_m_reduce_partials_kernel` — reduce partial M blocks into final M\n291\t- `precompute_m_eval_round_kernel` — extract `s'(1)`, `s'(2)` from M\n292\t- `multifold_kernel<W>` — multi-coordinate fold (templated on fold width)\n293\t\n294\t#### Strategy selection\n295\t\n296\t`choose_round_strategy()` picks precompute-M when all of the following hold:\n297\t- Not disabled via `SWIRL_CUDA_GKR_PRECOMPUTE_M=0`\n298\t- A valid window width `w` exists (currently `w = GKR_WINDOW_SIZE = 3`)\n299\t- `rem_n >= min_n`, where `min_n` defaults to `GKR_WINDOW_DEFAULT_MIN_N = 22` and can be overridden with `SWIRL_CUDA_GKR_PRECOMPUTE_M_MIN_N`\n300\t- At least `w` sumcheck rounds remain (`rounds_left >= w`)\n301\t- Estimated tail parallelism meets threshold: `num_tail_blocks(rem_n, w, tail_tile) >= min_blocks`, where `min_blocks` defaults to 64 (`SWIRL_CUDA_GKR_PRECOMPUTE_M_MIN_BLOCKS`)\n302\t\n303\t`tail_tile` is chosen adaptively from a target block count (default 1024, `SWIRL_CUDA_GKR_PRECOMPUTE_M_TARGET_BLOCKS`) and clamped to `[256, 4096]`, unless explicitly set by `SWIRL_CUDA_GKR_PRECOMPUTE_M_TAIL_TILE`.\n304\t\n305\tOtherwise it falls back to fold-eval.\n306\t\n307\t### Additional buffers\n308\t- `m_buffer` — the `4^w`-sized M matrix (`DeviceBuffer<EF>`)\n309\t- `m_partial_buffer` — partial M blocks from each CUDA thread block\n310\t- `eq_r_prefix_buffer`, `eq_suffix_buffer` — small device buffers for MLE evaluation tables (size `<= 2^w`)\n311\t- `d_sum_evals` — two `EF` values holding `s'(1)` and `s'(2)` from each round\n312\t- `tmp_block_sums` — block-level partial sums for reduction; reused for `eq_r_window` upload during multifold (safe because the two uses don't overlap temporally)\n313\t\n314\t### GPU memory accounting\n315\t\n316\tLet:\n317\t- `n = log2(total_leaves)`\n318\t- `w = GKR_WINDOW_SIZE` (default `w = 3`)\n319\t- `|EF| = sizeof(EF)` bytes\n320\t- `|Frac| = sizeof(Frac<EF>) = 2 * |EF|`\n321\t- Assume `n` is large\n322\t- Assume precompute-M is active\n323\t\n324\tBuffers used at peak (workspace only; excludes input `layer`/leaves):\n325\t- `work_buffer`: `2^(n-w-2) * |Frac|` (precompute-M defers `w+1` folds before first write)\n326\t- `copy_scratch`: `1 * |Frac|`\n327\t- `d_sum_evals`: `2 * |EF|`\n328\t- `tmp_block_sums`: `< 2^(n-7) * |EF|`\n329\t- `eq_buffer` (`SqrtEqLayers`): `(2^floor(n/2) + 2^ceil(n/2) - 2) * |EF|`\n330\t- `m_buffer`: `4^w * |EF|` (default `w=3` gives `64 * |EF|`)\n331\t- `m_partial_buffer`: `num_blocks * 4^w * |EF|`, with `num_blocks <= 2^(n-w-10)` for large `n`, so `< 2^(n+w-10) * |EF|`\n332\t- `eq_r_prefix_buffer` + `eq_suffix_buffer`: `2^(w+1) * |EF|` (default `w=3` gives `16 * |EF|`)\n333\t\n334\tWorkspace upper bound (sum of the buffers above), using `|Frac| = 2 * |EF|`:\n335\t- `W < (2^(n-w-1) + 2^(n-7) + 2^(n+w-10) + 2^floor(n/2) + 2^ceil(n/2) + 4^w + 2^(w+1) + 2) * |EF|`\n336\t\n337\tTotal GPU memory required (workspace + input leaves `2^n * |Frac| = 2^(n+1) * |EF|`):\n338\t- `M_total < (2^(n+1) + 2^(n-w-1) + 2^(n-7) + 2^(n+w-10) + 2^floor(n/2) + 2^ceil(n/2) + 4^w + 2^(w+1) + 2) * |EF|`\n339\t- With default `w = 3` and `|EF| = 16` bytes:\n340\t  - `M_total < (2^(n+1) + 2^(n-4) + 2^(n-6) + 2^floor(n/2) + 2^ceil(n/2) + 82) / 2^26 GiB`\n341\t  - `n = 27`: `M_total < 4.16 GiB`\n342\t  - `n = 28`: `M_total < 8.31 GiB`\n343\t  - `n = 29`: `M_total < 16.63 GiB`\n344\t  - `n = 30`: `M_total < 33.25 GiB`\n345\t\n346\tDominant term is the input leaves (`2^(n+1) * |EF|`). Workspace overhead is ~4%.\n347\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Dp5KkZAhmqRxX8kHPvjXQH","session_id":"794cedb4-74b2-4dd2-b512-5aabd1b44ebc","uuid":"1c9eac8f-fb61-4069-8014-69306f0a3908","timestamp":"2026-04-13T16:40:09.561Z"}
