{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Dp5KkZAhmqRxX8kHPvjXQH","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have enough information to provide a comprehensive answer. Let me compile the report.\n\n## GKR Protocol Code Path Analysis\n\nBased on my investigation of the CUDA backend, here's a detailed analysis of the LogUp GKR fractional sumcheck protocol implementation:\n\n### 1. **fractional_sumcheck_gpu Function Overview**\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs:491-1050`\n\nThis is the main entry point. Key structure:\n- Called **once per proof** (line 374 in `mod.rs`), not per-AIR\n- Operates on **all accumulated interactions in bulk** as a single batch\n- Input: `DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>` containing `2^n` leaf pairs (p, q) from all AIRs\n- Output: Fractional sum proof + randomness vector `ξ`\n\n**Protocol flow:**\n1. **Segment tree build** (lines 517-576): Builds tree bottom-up in place\n2. **Main GKR loop** (lines 655-1050): Runs `total_rounds = log2(total_leaves)` rounds\n3. Each round processes variables systematically, not per-AIR\n\n### 2. **Total Rounds & Architecture**\n\n**Number of rounds:** `total_rounds = log2(total_leaves)` where `total_leaves = 2^n` leaf pairs\n\nFor APC 300 with typical LogUp interactions (~300 interactions × 2^20 rows), you get:\n- `log2(2^20) = 20` GKR rounds\n- Each round runs `j` sumcheck rounds (1 ≤ j ≤ 19)\n- Round `j` has `j-1` inner sumcheck rounds\n\n**Rounds are NOT per-AIR:** All AIRs' interactions are **flattened and batched** into a single leaf buffer. The GKR prover works on this bulk data, which is more efficient than per-AIR processing.\n\n### 3. **Per-Round GPU Work - Two Strategies**\n\n**Strategy A: Fold-Eval (lines 717-820)**\nProcesses one variable per inner round:\n- **compute_round_and_revert_kernel** (line 371, line 1037 in CUDA): First round - reverts tree layer + computes s'(1,2)\n- **compute_round_and_fold_kernel** or **compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel** (lines 419, 465): Fuses variable fold + poly evaluation\n- **fold_ef_columns_kernel** (line 798): Final standalone fold\n- **Block reduction kernels** to aggregate partial sums\n\nEach loop iteration:\n```rust\nfor &xi_j in xi_prev.iter().skip(1) {  // lines 721-792\n    // One of 4 variants called per iteration\n    let r = do_fused_sumcheck_round(...)?;\n    pq_size >>= 1;\n}\n```\n\n**Strategy B: Precompute-M (lines 821-1003)**\nProcesses `w=3` variables at once (lines 1-3 of the window):\n- **precompute_m_build_partial_kernel** (line 910): Builds M matrix for window\n- **precompute_m_reduce_partials_kernel**: Reduces partial blocks\n- **precompute_m_eval_round_kernel** (line 944): Extracts s'(1,2) from M, called w times per window\n- **multifold_kernel** (line 988): Multi-variable fold with deferred challenges\n\nWindow loop:\n```rust\nwhile base < stop {  // lines 853-1003\n    // Build M from tail points\n    frac_precompute_m_build_raw(...)?;  // line 910\n    \n    // For each of w variables:\n    for t in 0..w {  // lines 929-966\n        frac_precompute_m_eval_round_raw(...)?;  // line 944\n        eq_buffer.drop_layer();\n        let r = observe_and_update(...)?;  // line 955 - CPU SYNC POINT\n    }\n    \n    // Fold w+1 variables at once\n    frac_multifold_raw(...)?;  // line 988\n    base += w;\n}\n```\n\n### 4. **Key GPU Kernels & Their Purpose**\n\n**Tree build kernels** (`frac_build_tree_layer_kernel`, `frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel`):\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr.cu:28-857`\n- Fractional addition: `(L.p * R.q + R.p * L.q, L.q * R.q)`\n- Fused K=2: combines two layers in one pass (~33% bandwidth savings)\n\n**Sumcheck computation** (`compute_round_block_sum_kernel`):\n- Lines 135-200 in gkr.cu\n- Accumulates `s'(1)` and `s'(2)` evaluations\n- Uses eq buffer reconstruction from sqrt decomposition\n- Per-block partial sums reduced on CPU\n\n**Precompute-M kernels** (lines 515-695 in gkr.cu):\n- `precompute_m_build_partial_kernel<inline_fold, W>`: O(2^{n+w}) work over tail points\n- `precompute_m_eval_round_kernel`: O(2^{2w}) work, small enough to fit in L2\n- `multifold_kernel<W>`: Fold w coordinates in one pass\n\n### 5. **CPU-GPU Synchronization Points**\n\n**Per-round synchronization** (critical for 214ms analysis):\n\n1. **observe_and_update function** (lines 318-346 in fractional.rs):\n   ```rust\n   let sp_vec = d_sum_evals.to_host()?;  // GPU->CPU: 2 EF values\n   ```\n   - Copies 2 EF values (32 bytes) from device per inner round\n   - Reconstructs s(1,2,3) evaluations on CPU\n   - Samples new challenge `r` and updates accumulators\n\n2. **For each GKR round** (line 655 loop):\n   - Creates new `SqrtEqLayers` on CPU (line 663)\n   - Samples `lambda` (line 670)\n   - Calls one root GPU kernel per inner round\n\n3. **Per-window in Precompute-M** (lines 929-966):\n   - **w calls to observe_and_update** (line 955) - w=3 means 3 GPU->CPU transfers per window\n   - Each transfer: 2 EF values (~32 bytes)\n   - Not a bottleneck (negligible vs kernel time)\n\n### 6. **Per-AIR Overhead Analysis**\n\n**GOOD NEWS: Per-AIR overhead is minimal**\n\nThe GKR input preparation (`log_gkr_input_evals`, lines 215-354 in gkr_input.rs) has per-AIR work:\n- Processes AIRs in parallel across multiple CUDA streams (lines 284-349)\n- Pre-allocates buffers to avoid mutex contention\n- Each AIR: reads interactions, computes contributions, writes to leaf buffer\n\n**However, this is NOT inside the GKR main loop.** It happens once before `fractional_sumcheck_gpu` is called. The main GKR protocol (which takes 214ms) operates on **already-aggregated bulk data**.\n\n**Key point from line 351-352 in gkr_input.rs:**\n```rust\n// NOTE: alpha is NO LONGER applied here - it will be fused into the first tree layer\n// in fractional_sumcheck_gpu for better performance\n```\n\nAlpha application is fused into the first tree layer kernel, eliminating separate passes.\n\n### 7. **GPU Memory & Work Organization**\n\n**Bulk operations (no per-AIR loops):**\n- Segment tree: single buffer, in-place operations\n- Each GKR round: operates on `2^n` elements, folded progressively\n- Precompute-M: fixed window size w=3, O(2^{n+w}) work regardless of num_interactions\n\n**Workspace:** Pre-allocated once per proof, reused across all rounds\n- `work_buffer`: `2^(n-w-2)` elements\n- `m_buffer`: `4^w = 64` EF (for w=3)\n- `eq_buffer`: sqrt-decomposed, ~`2^(n/2)` elements\n\n### 8. **Optimization Opportunities for 214ms Protocol**\n\n**Potential batching/overlap opportunities:**\n\n1. **Precompute-M activation threshold**: Currently activates at `rem_n >= 22` (line 299, `GKR_WINDOW_DEFAULT_MIN_N`). At APC 300 with n=20, this may not activate. Check: `SWIRL_CUDA_GKR_PRECOMPUTE_M=0` env var control (line 619).\n\n2. **Fold-Eval vs Precompute-M trade-off**: At APC 300 (n~20), the fold-eval strategy may dominate. Precompute-M has 2^w/w arithmetic overhead but reduces memory bandwidth—profile to see which dominates.\n\n3. **CPU-GPU synchronization is already minimal**: The observe_and_update transfers are tiny (32 bytes per round). No major batching opportunity here.\n\n4. **Eq buffer computation**: Re-computed per GKR round on CPU (line 663), but this is amortized since it's O(n) work per GKR round, not per sumcheck round.\n\n5. **Kernel launch overhead**: Multiple small kernels per round (especially in Precompute-M with w separate eval rounds). Fusing eval rounds into a single kernel could help, but likely not the bottleneck at 214ms.\n\n**Likely bottleneck:** Memory bandwidth in fold-eval rounds. Each compute_round_and_fold reads input buffer and writes output buffer. At n=20, this is 2^20 elements × 2 passes × 2 FpExt per element = significant traffic.\n\n---\n\n**File References:**\n- Main protocol: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs:491-1050`\n- CUDA kernels: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr.cu`\n- GKR input prep: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs:215-354`\n- Documentation: `/home/georg/stark-backend/docs/cuda-backend/gkr-prover.md`"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"794cedb4-74b2-4dd2-b512-5aabd1b44ebc","uuid":"0435e844-aa68-4b74-8d8c-80c2c2521a6b","timestamp":"2026-04-13T16:41:03.619Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I'm analyzing GPU prover performance in a STARK backend. The LogUp GKR protocol (fractional_sumcheck) takes ~214ms at APC 300 and hasn't been optimized yet.\n\nI need to understand the GKR protocol code path in the cuda-backend:\n1. The `fractional_sumcheck_gpu` function — how it works, what it calls\n2. The key GKR kernels: `compute_round_and_revert_kernel`, `frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel`, `bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel`, `multifold_kernel`, `precompute_m_build_partial_kernel`\n3. How many rounds the protocol runs and what work is done per round\n4. Whether there are per-AIR or per-trace iterations within the protocol (as opposed to bulk GPU operations)\n5. Any synchronization points or sequential dependencies between rounds\n\nLook in:\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/` for Rust code\n- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/` for CUDA kernels\n- Search for `fractional_sumcheck`, `gkr_rounds`, `frac_build`, `multifold`\n\nThoroughness: medium. Report file paths and line numbers. I want to understand if there's per-AIR overhead that could be batched, or if the protocol is already operating on bulk data.","agentId":"a8363526c7624224e","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have enough information to provide a comprehensive answer. Let me compile the report.\n\n## GKR Protocol Code Path Analysis\n\nBased on my investigation of the CUDA backend, here's a detailed analysis of the LogUp GKR fractional sumcheck protocol implementation:\n\n### 1. **fractional_sumcheck_gpu Function Overview**\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs:491-1050`\n\nThis is the main entry point. Key structure:\n- Called **once per proof** (line 374 in `mod.rs`), not per-AIR\n- Operates on **all accumulated interactions in bulk** as a single batch\n- Input: `DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>` containing `2^n` leaf pairs (p, q) from all AIRs\n- Output: Fractional sum proof + randomness vector `ξ`\n\n**Protocol flow:**\n1. **Segment tree build** (lines 517-576): Builds tree bottom-up in place\n2. **Main GKR loop** (lines 655-1050): Runs `total_rounds = log2(total_leaves)` rounds\n3. Each round processes variables systematically, not per-AIR\n\n### 2. **Total Rounds & Architecture**\n\n**Number of rounds:** `total_rounds = log2(total_leaves)` where `total_leaves = 2^n` leaf pairs\n\nFor APC 300 with typical LogUp interactions (~300 interactions × 2^20 rows), you get:\n- `log2(2^20) = 20` GKR rounds\n- Each round runs `j` sumcheck rounds (1 ≤ j ≤ 19)\n- Round `j` has `j-1` inner sumcheck rounds\n\n**Rounds are NOT per-AIR:** All AIRs' interactions are **flattened and batched** into a single leaf buffer. The GKR prover works on this bulk data, which is more efficient than per-AIR processing.\n\n### 3. **Per-Round GPU Work - Two Strategies**\n\n**Strategy A: Fold-Eval (lines 717-820)**\nProcesses one variable per inner round:\n- **compute_round_and_revert_kernel** (line 371, line 1037 in CUDA): First round - reverts tree layer + computes s'(1,2)\n- **compute_round_and_fold_kernel** or **compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel** (lines 419, 465): Fuses variable fold + poly evaluation\n- **fold_ef_columns_kernel** (line 798): Final standalone fold\n- **Block reduction kernels** to aggregate partial sums\n\nEach loop iteration:\n```rust\nfor &xi_j in xi_prev.iter().skip(1) {  // lines 721-792\n    // One of 4 variants called per iteration\n    let r = do_fused_sumcheck_round(...)?;\n    pq_size >>= 1;\n}\n```\n\n**Strategy B: Precompute-M (lines 821-1003)**\nProcesses `w=3` variables at once (lines 1-3 of the window):\n- **precompute_m_build_partial_kernel** (line 910): Builds M matrix for window\n- **precompute_m_reduce_partials_kernel**: Reduces partial blocks\n- **precompute_m_eval_round_kernel** (line 944): Extracts s'(1,2) from M, called w times per window\n- **multifold_kernel** (line 988): Multi-variable fold with deferred challenges\n\nWindow loop:\n```rust\nwhile base < stop {  // lines 853-1003\n    // Build M from tail points\n    frac_precompute_m_build_raw(...)?;  // line 910\n    \n    // For each of w variables:\n    for t in 0..w {  // lines 929-966\n        frac_precompute_m_eval_round_raw(...)?;  // line 944\n        eq_buffer.drop_layer();\n        let r = observe_and_update(...)?;  // line 955 - CPU SYNC POINT\n    }\n    \n    // Fold w+1 variables at once\n    frac_multifold_raw(...)?;  // line 988\n    base += w;\n}\n```\n\n### 4. **Key GPU Kernels & Their Purpose**\n\n**Tree build kernels** (`frac_build_tree_layer_kernel`, `frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel`):\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr.cu:28-857`\n- Fractional addition: `(L.p * R.q + R.p * L.q, L.q * R.q)`\n- Fused K=2: combines two layers in one pass (~33% bandwidth savings)\n\n**Sumcheck computation** (`compute_round_block_sum_kernel`):\n- Lines 135-200 in gkr.cu\n- Accumulates `s'(1)` and `s'(2)` evaluations\n- Uses eq buffer reconstruction from sqrt decomposition\n- Per-block partial sums reduced on CPU\n\n**Precompute-M kernels** (lines 515-695 in gkr.cu):\n- `precompute_m_build_partial_kernel<inline_fold, W>`: O(2^{n+w}) work over tail points\n- `precompute_m_eval_round_kernel`: O(2^{2w}) work, small enough to fit in L2\n- `multifold_kernel<W>`: Fold w coordinates in one pass\n\n### 5. **CPU-GPU Synchronization Points**\n\n**Per-round synchronization** (critical for 214ms analysis):\n\n1. **observe_and_update function** (lines 318-346 in fractional.rs):\n   ```rust\n   let sp_vec = d_sum_evals.to_host()?;  // GPU->CPU: 2 EF values\n   ```\n   - Copies 2 EF values (32 bytes) from device per inner round\n   - Reconstructs s(1,2,3) evaluations on CPU\n   - Samples new challenge `r` and updates accumulators\n\n2. **For each GKR round** (line 655 loop):\n   - Creates new `SqrtEqLayers` on CPU (line 663)\n   - Samples `lambda` (line 670)\n   - Calls one root GPU kernel per inner round\n\n3. **Per-window in Precompute-M** (lines 929-966):\n   - **w calls to observe_and_update** (line 955) - w=3 means 3 GPU->CPU transfers per window\n   - Each transfer: 2 EF values (~32 bytes)\n   - Not a bottleneck (negligible vs kernel time)\n\n### 6. **Per-AIR Overhead Analysis**\n\n**GOOD NEWS: Per-AIR overhead is minimal**\n\nThe GKR input preparation (`log_gkr_input_evals`, lines 215-354 in gkr_input.rs) has per-AIR work:\n- Processes AIRs in parallel across multiple CUDA streams (lines 284-349)\n- Pre-allocates buffers to avoid mutex contention\n- Each AIR: reads interactions, computes contributions, writes to leaf buffer\n\n**However, this is NOT inside the GKR main loop.** It happens once before `fractional_sumcheck_gpu` is called. The main GKR protocol (which takes 214ms) operates on **already-aggregated bulk data**.\n\n**Key point from line 351-352 in gkr_input.rs:**\n```rust\n// NOTE: alpha is NO LONGER applied here - it will be fused into the first tree layer\n// in fractional_sumcheck_gpu for better performance\n```\n\nAlpha application is fused into the first tree layer kernel, eliminating separate passes.\n\n### 7. **GPU Memory & Work Organization**\n\n**Bulk operations (no per-AIR loops):**\n- Segment tree: single buffer, in-place operations\n- Each GKR round: operates on `2^n` elements, folded progressively\n- Precompute-M: fixed window size w=3, O(2^{n+w}) work regardless of num_interactions\n\n**Workspace:** Pre-allocated once per proof, reused across all rounds\n- `work_buffer`: `2^(n-w-2)` elements\n- `m_buffer`: `4^w = 64` EF (for w=3)\n- `eq_buffer`: sqrt-decomposed, ~`2^(n/2)` elements\n\n### 8. **Optimization Opportunities for 214ms Protocol**\n\n**Potential batching/overlap opportunities:**\n\n1. **Precompute-M activation threshold**: Currently activates at `rem_n >= 22` (line 299, `GKR_WINDOW_DEFAULT_MIN_N`). At APC 300 with n=20, this may not activate. Check: `SWIRL_CUDA_GKR_PRECOMPUTE_M=0` env var control (line 619).\n\n2. **Fold-Eval vs Precompute-M trade-off**: At APC 300 (n~20), the fold-eval strategy may dominate. Precompute-M has 2^w/w arithmetic overhead but reduces memory bandwidth—profile to see which dominates.\n\n3. **CPU-GPU synchronization is already minimal**: The observe_and_update transfers are tiny (32 bytes per round). No major batching opportunity here.\n\n4. **Eq buffer computation**: Re-computed per GKR round on CPU (line 663), but this is amortized since it's O(n) work per GKR round, not per sumcheck round.\n\n5. **Kernel launch overhead**: Multiple small kernels per round (especially in Precompute-M with w separate eval rounds). Fusing eval rounds into a single kernel could help, but likely not the bottleneck at 214ms.\n\n**Likely bottleneck:** Memory bandwidth in fold-eval rounds. Each compute_round_and_fold reads input buffer and writes output buffer. At n=20, this is 2^20 elements × 2 passes × 2 FpExt per element = significant traffic.\n\n---\n\n**File References:**\n- Main protocol: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs:491-1050`\n- CUDA kernels: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr.cu`\n- GKR input prep: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs:215-354`\n- Documentation: `/home/georg/stark-backend/docs/cuda-backend/gkr-prover.md`"}],"totalDurationMs":86902,"totalTokens":63537,"totalToolUseCount":37,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1754,"cache_read_input_tokens":59124,"output_tokens":2657,"server_tool