tially making the critical path worse rather than better.\n\nLet me reconsider what's actually limiting performance here. The wall time is 301ms with 509ms of total kernel time, giving only 1.69x concurrency instead of the ideal 8x with 8 threads. The real bottleneck isn't the large AIRs saturating the GPU—it's that each thread processes its ~38 AIRs sequentially, launching a kernel, waiting for data transfer, processing on CPU, then repeating. Even with multiple threads, this sequential pattern within each thread is what's constraining the overall throughput.\n\nThe current load balancing strategy sorts AIRs by height and distributes them evenly, which means Thread 0 gets the largest AIRs and becomes the slowest thread. Since wall time is determined by whichever thread finishes last, I need to either redistribute those large AIRs more evenly across threads, reduce the per-AIR overhead for them, or change how they're processed entirely.\n\nThe real issue is that the largest AIRs have exponentially more work than smaller ones—if heights follow powers of 2, the first chunk concentrates all the heaviest work. Instead of chunking sorted AIRs, I should use round-robin assignment: alternate assigning AIRs across threads so each thread gets a mix of large and small ones. This spreads the computational load much more evenly.\n\nNow I'm working through the actual numbers. The 20 largest AIRs probably account for most of the 509ms total kernel time. If I distribute those 300ms across 8 threads, that's about 38ms per thread, plus roughly 26ms from the smaller AIRs, giving around 64ms per thread before accounting for data transfer and CPU overhead. Adding those in, the critical thread would finish around 94ms—a significant improvement over the current bottleneck.\n\nThe real win here is that while the large kernels still can't run concurrently on the GPU due to saturation, the data transfers and CPU post-processing for one thread can overlap with kernel execution from another thread. That's where the parallelism actually helps. Let me recalculate what the current sequential approach actually costs per thread to see if my estimate holds up. can't be right because total Round 0 is only 301ms.\n\nLet me recalculate. Thread 0 has the 38 largest AIRs. If average kernel time per large AIR is (509ms / 620 AIRs) × some factor = 0.82ms average (many small, few large). For the top 38, the average is much higher.\n\nActually, I realize the issue. The per-AIR kernel time distribution is very skewed. A few AIRs (height 2^20) have 10-16ms kernel times, but most have <0.5ms. Even thread 0's 38 \"largest\" AIRs include mostly medium-sized ones.\n\nLooking at the height distribution, I'd expect the AIRs to cluster around a few ranges: the tallest ones at 2^17-2^20 with 10-16ms kernel times each, a middle band at 2^12-2^16 with a few milliseconds per AIR, and the bulk of them at 2^4-2^10 with just microseconds. Thread 0 gets the top 38 which should average around 5ms each across both kernels, giving roughly 190ms total, while thread 7's bottom 38 are all small traces taking maybe 0.2ms per AIR plus overhead.\n\nWith round-robin distribution, each thread would get a mix of large and small AIRs, balancing the load to around 32ms per thread plus overhead. But I'm realizing the GPU execution is serialized by SM saturation, so even with better thread load balancing, the kernels still run sequentially on the GPU.\n\nThe total GPU time of 509ms is fixed, but what matters is how much D2H transfer and CPU overhead can overlap with GPU compute. Large AIRs run sequentially for about 300ms, while small AIRs can overlap and take roughly 26ms, giving a total GPU time around 326ms where some of the D2H and CPU work can hide behind the compute.\n\nWith round-robin scheduling, the overlap would improve significantly because while one thread's GPU kernel runs, another thread can transfer results and process them on the CPU. The key difference is that chunking keeps all of one thread's AIRs on the same stream running sequentially, whereas round-robin would interleave large and small AIRs across threads, letting CPU work fill the gaps between GPU kernels. 8 streams allow different threads' kernels to overlap on the GPU. But the CPU processing within each thread creates gaps between kernel launches on that thread's stream.\n\nThe critical bottleneck is Thread 0's total time: sum of (GPU_kernel_time + D2H + CPU_processing) for all 38 AIRs. If GPU kernels average 5ms and overhead averages 1ms per AIR, Thread 0 takes 38 × 6ms = 228ms. Other threads have less work but still contribute some wall time.\n\nWith round-robin distribution, each thread gets roughly equal kernel time (509/8 = 64ms) plus overhead (38ms), bringing Thread 0 down to around 102ms — better than 228ms because the kernel times are more evenly spread.\n\nBut here's the catch: when Thread 0 launches a large 5ms kernel, it saturates the GPU and blocks other threads from running theirs concurrently. The large kernels effectively serialize across threads regardless of distribution. So the GPU execution time is dominated by the large kernels running sequentially (~300ms), with small kernels overlapping where possible (~26ms), totaling around 326ms. Adding the per-thread overhead of about 39ms on top of that, the wall time is still constrained by this GPU serialization effect.\n\nThe real question is whether round-robin can meaningfully reduce the 301ms Round 0 time. The answer is probably not significantly, because that 301ms is driven by the fixed GPU critical path of large AIRs that must run sequentially, plus the per-AIR overhead. Round-robin redistributes the overhead evenly across threads, but it doesn't change the fundamental GPU bottleneck.\n\nLooking at the math: with chunking, Thread 0 carries both the longest GPU kernels and most overhead (~319ms total, though GPU overlap brings it to ~301ms). With round-robin, each thread gets less GPU work (~41ms) plus the same per-thread overhead (~19ms), but the GPU serialization constraint means the actual time is still dominated by the critical path (~326ms+), making it potentially worse since there's less GPU work to overlap with the overhead.\n\nLet me step back and think about this more directly. The real constraint is the GPU compute time (~509ms), which can't be reduced. The D2H copies and CPU post-processing add another ~310ms combined, but some of that overlaps naturally with GPU work. The current approach achieves ~301ms, suggesting about 208ms of overlap. The theoretical minimum would be limited by whichever is larger: the GPU compute or the non-overlappable portions of the other work.\n\nThe key insight is that with 8 streams, the GPU is effectively running about 1.69 kernels concurrently on average (509ms ÷ 301ms), which means large kernels saturate the SMs while smaller ones can run a few at a time. So we're already pretty close to the theoretical limit for this architecture.\n\nSince Round 0 is GPU-bound with D2H and CPU processing fully overlapped, the only real levers are optimizing individual kernels, reducing total GPU compute, or batching small AIRs to cut down on launch overhead. If we could batch those ~280 small AIRs into just 2 kernel launches instead of 560, we'd save most of that 209ms of small AIR compute time, bringing total GPU time down to around 326ms—though the batched kernel itself would need to run efficiently.\n\nBut wait, if the large AIR kernels are already saturating the GPU, the batched small AIR kernel can't run concurrently anyway, so we'd just be adding 26ms on top of the 300ms from large AIRs, which is actually worse than the current 301ms where small kernels sneak in between large ones during their D2H phases. The fine-grained interleaving we get now would be lost with batching.\n\nI think Round 0 optimization is hitting a wall—301ms is already pretty close to the theoretical minimum for the GPU work involved. Real improvements would require making the kernels themselves faster, which is a much harder problem. Let me shift focus to the other rounds.\n\nLooking at LogUp GKR's 526ms breakdown, the input evals are taking 307ms across two segments—244ms for the large AIRs which are kernel-dominated, and 63ms for the small AIRs which are already well-optimized. The protocol phase is another 214ms. For that 244ms segment, the large AIR kernel instances total 461ms of wall time, and with 8 streams providing roughly 2x effective concurrency, that should compress down to around 230ms, which matches the measured 244ms pretty closely. So the GKR input eval is also GPU-compute-bound.\n\nThe protocol phase itself is a sequential operation with substantial kernels—bit reversal building, multifold, precompute, and round computation kernels are all eating significant time. The protocol structure has about 20 GKR rounds with inner sumcheck rounds, and each round depends on the previous one, so there's inherent sequential dependency. But I should check whether there's any parallelism possible within individual rounds.\n\nLooking at the fractional sumcheck approach more carefully, each round follows a pattern: compute the round polynomial by evaluating at a few points, send it to the verifier (which is just a transcript operation), receive a challenge, then fold the MLE. The GPU work happens in steps 1 and 4, while steps 2 and 3 are negligible CPU operations. The synchronization overhead from reading back 32 bytes per round is minimal, so the bottleneck is purely the GPU compute and memory bandwidth of those kernel operations themselves.\n\nFor the MLE rounds at 170ms, they're already batched, so what remains is the actual GPU evaluation work—there's not much more optimization headroom without diving into kernel-level improvements.\n\nNow I'm examining the nsys profile to see where the time is actually spent across all the different kernels—the zerocheck and logup batch operations are taking the most time, followed by the precompute combinations kernels and the monomial kernels.\n\nThe total GPU compute is about 158ms out of 170ms wall time, so we're at 93% utilization with minimal overhead. The precompute_logup_combinations kernels are called 791 times across roughly 310 traces and 2.5 rounds on average, which works out to about 47 microseconds per call. I'm wondering if there's an opportunity to batch these operations since they're currently being called per-trace, even though the main evaluation kernels already batch across all AIRs in a single launch.\n\nLooking at the numbers more carefully, those 791 precompute calls total about 37ms, which is 22% of the MLE Rounds time. If I batched them into one kernel per round, I'd save some launch overhead—around 4ms from eliminating 791 separate launches—but the actual compute work would remain the same.\n\nLet me refocus on what matters. The current STARK execution at APC 300 is 1433ms, which is 1.71x better than the baseline 2455ms, but at APC 0 it regressed to 2654ms from the baseline 2153ms. The remaining bottlenecks at APC 300 are all GPU-compute-bound, with Round 0 alone taking 301ms on the GPU.\n\nMost of the per-AIR overhead is gone now, so what's left is genuine GPU compute work. To push further, I'd need either faster kernels, less GPU work through algorithmic changes, or better GPU utilization. But given the constraints around protocol and verifier changes, my options are limited. The one area I haven't fully explored yet is the Trace Commit RS code matrix computation.\n\nBreaking down the Trace Commit work: the scatter kernel takes about 16ms, the RS code matrix computation with NTT and polynomial evaluation is the unknown, and then there's the merkle tree hashing and construction for the remainder. If RS code matrix is eating up significant time, that could be worth optimizing.\n\nLooking at the per-segment breakdown, stacked_commit shows seg0 at 163ms and seg1 at 87ms, with rs_code_matrix_time hitting 57ms in seg0. That means the remaining 106ms is split between the scatter kernel and merkle tree work. I need to check if I have the seg1 value for RS code matrix to get the full picture.\n\nFrom the kernel profiling data, I can see the NTT operations are substantial—the _CT_NTT kernels are running for 82ms and 30ms across multiple instances, plus bit reversal permutation at 33ms and batch NTT at 32ms. These are spread across both the app proof and recursion phases, so I need to isolate just the app proof contribution to understand where the real bottleneck is.\n\nI'm realizing I've been going in circles analyzing these numbers instead of taking action. The pattern from past optimizations is clear: per-AIR overhead reductions have been the biggest wins, but those opportunities are mostly tapped out now. What's left is fundamentally GPU-compute-bound work. The next real gains have to come from making the kernels themselves faster or reducing the total GPU work. But before I commit to that direction, I should investigate the compute_merkle_precomputation_cuda_time_ms metric more carefully—that might be another per-AIR overhead I haven't fully explored yet.\n\nActually, what if the real opportunity is reducing the number of committed traces altogether? The preprocessed traces are committed at keygen, not during proving, but there could be per-AIR cached main commits that each spawn their own expensive Merkle tree. Looking at the metrics though, I only see 2 main_trace_commit calls across the segments, so those per-AIR commits might not even be happening at APC 300.\n\nI need to stop deliberating and pick a direction. The trace commit work is already 255ms and is fundamentally limited by GPU hashing performance. Round 0 is sitting at 301ms.\n\nActually, wait—I haven't fully explored the Openings phase yet. WHIR is the polynomial commitment opening protocol, and it's only 101ms at APC 300 but jumps to 292ms at APC 0. Let me dig into what's actually happening there.\n\nLooking at the kernel breakdown, I see whir_algebraic_batch_traces_kernel taking 18ms, then smaller kernels for folding coefficients and sumcheck rounds, plus Merkle tree operations and grind_kernel at 18ms. These are all relatively small individual operations, so 101ms total isn't really a great optimization target.\n\nI think the real opportunity here is stepping back from optimizing individual phases and instead looking at **reducing CPU-GPU synchronization across the entire proving pipeline**. Rather than waiting for one phase to fully complete before starting the next, I could potentially overlap work—like starting openings preparation while the CPU is still processing constraint results, or pipelining segment processing so segment 1's constraint evaluation runs while segment 0 is being opened.\n\nThese are higher-level architectural changes that could reduce wall time without touching individual kernels, though they'd require understanding the full prover flow.\n\nLet me actually look more carefully at the metrics data and check if there's a gap between the sum of sub-phases and the total. At APC 300, the STARK phase excluding trace is 1433ms, which breaks down to Constraints (999ms), Openings (177ms), Trace Commit (255ms), and Other (2ms). Adding those up gives exactly 1433ms with no gap, which tells me these phases are running sequentially.\n\nDrilling into Constraints at 999ms, I see LogUp GKR at 526ms, Round 0 at 301ms, MLE Rounds at 170ms, and Other at 2ms—again summing to exactly 999ms with no overlap. So the entire STARK phase is a strict sequence, meaning any improvement to any component directly reduces the total time.\n\nLooking at the biggest remaining bottlenecks, LogUp GKR dominates at 526ms, followed by Round 0 at 301ms, Trace Commit at 255ms, Openings at 177ms, and MLE Rounds at 170ms. To hit a 100ms improvement target, I'd need about 7% savings somewhere. The Round 0 load balancing idea is worth revisiting—even if the GPU critical path is fixed, there might be better CPU-side optimization opportunities.\n\nFor Round 0 specifically, the GPU compute time of 509ms with ~1.69 effective concurrency maps to about 301ms, and the D2H plus CPU processing is fully hidden behind that GPU work. But if I could reduce the GPU kernel time itself, the overall Round 0 time would drop. The kernel time is dominated by constraint evaluation and interaction computation across all AIRs, which is the fundamental work required. The only way to reduce it is to avoid some of that work entirely—through lazy evaluation to skip AIRs with zero constraints, caching intermediate results across segments, or early termination when results are known.\n\nI need to stop going in circles and commit to an approach. My best option is to parallelize the MLE Rounds evaluation across multiple GPU streams instead of running them sequentially on the default stream. Currently, each of the ~16 rounds runs a batched interpolation kernel followed by sequential per-trace evaluations for both late and early cases, then a fold and reduce. The interpolation kernel already handles all AIRs in one launch, but the per-trace work is still sequential—distributing that across streams could unlock better GPU utilization.\n\nLooking back at the batch-mle-round-kernels optimization, it consolidated ~15K per-AIR kernel launches into just 2 batched launches per round using descriptor arrays. But examining the nsys profile, the sumcheck evaluation kernels like zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel and logup_batch_mle_kernel still process traces one at a time, even though they're already batched across multiple instances.\n\nThe real bottleneck appears to be the precompute_logup_combinations kernels firing 791 times per trace per round — that's 37ms total with about 4ms just from launch overhead alone. These tiny per-trace kernel launches are the next optimization target.\n\nLooking at the bigger picture, batching these precompute operations into single descriptor-array kernels per round could save 20-35ms from the MLE rounds, and there are similar opportunities with other per-trace operations like fold_ple_from_evals that fire 797 times. This batching approach has already proven successful for per-AIR operations, so it's worth pursuing here. Looking at the remaining kernel metrics, I need to be more careful about which kernels actually belong to the MLE Rounds phase versus the stacked_reduction or other phases. The `stacked_reduction_*` kernels are clearly from the stacked_reduction openings, not MLE Rounds, so I should exclude those. Let me focus on what I can definitively attribute to MLE Rounds—the precompute kernels and the fold operations that are specifically part of the evaluation path. Looking at the performance breakdown, the per-trace kernels are consuming 81ms—over half the MLE Rounds time—despite being called thousands of times with microsecond-level execution. If I batch these into descriptor-array kernels, I could potentially cut the total MLE Rounds time from 170ms down to 110-130ms, gaining 40-60ms of improvement.\n\nThis batching approach has already proven effective for interpolation, degenerate/nondegenerate rounds, and stacking operations, so applying it to the monomial evaluation and logup combination kernels should follow the same pattern. The complexity is moderate since I'm working with multiple kernel types, but the high probability of success and the significant performance gains make this my top optimization candidate.\n\nBeyond that, I'm also considering whether batching the precompute kernels alongside the per-trace evaluation kernels could yield even better results, since both operate on the same per-trace data.\n\nLooking more closely at the evaluation kernels themselves, I notice the `zerocheck_monomial_par_y_kernel` and `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` variants handle different AIR types—the par_y version processes larger AIRs with parallel-y optimization while the regular variant handles standard AIRs. The par_y instances average 418μs each across 43 calls, whereas the regular variant averages 96μs across 178 calls, which makes sense given the difference in data volume they're processing.\n\nTo batch these effectively, I'd need to construct a descriptor array that groups instances by kernel type.\n\nLet me look at the actual per-instance costs more carefully. The precompute_logup_combinations kernels are running at 30μs and 16μs per instance respectively, precompute_lambda_combinations at just 4μs, the zerocheck variants at 96-418μs, and the logup_monomial kernels at 22μs and lower—these numbers help clarify which kernels would benefit most from batching.\n\nThe precompute kernels are so lightweight that kernel launch overhead dominates the cost. Consolidating 791 launches would eliminate roughly 4ms of overhead per kernel type, saving around 12ms total across all precompute operations. However, since the actual compute time is only about 28ms, the net gain would be modest at around 12ms.\n\nThe monomial evaluation kernels are heavier, so launch overhead is less dominant relative to their compute time, meaning batching them would yield smaller percentage improvements—roughly 0.9ms for zerocheck_monomial and 0.2ms for zerocheck_monomial_par_y.\n\nBeyond just launch overhead, reducing the number of kernel launches from ~3000 to ~300 would cut CPU-side overhead like function call setup and FFI boundary crossing, potentially saving another 20ms. Combined with the GPU-side savings, I'm looking at roughly 35ms of total improvement, which would bring MLE Rounds down from 170ms to around 135ms.\n\nThat clears the 10ms threshold for MLE Rounds improvements, though it's not a dramatic win. But wait — I'm noticing that the batched evaluation kernels like `zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel` and `logup_batch_mle_kernel` are already optimized, yet the precompute and monomial evaluation kernels are still being called per-trace. Those precompute calls are preparing data that feeds into the batch kernels, so there might be an opportunity to batch those operations too.\n\nLooking at the code structure, I see there are actually two separate evaluation paths running in parallel — one using monomial-form evaluation and another using DAG-form evaluation. The precompute kernels belong to the monomial path while the batch MLE kernels handle the DAG path, which means I need to understand which path is the bottleneck before deciding where to focus optimization effort.\n\nNow examining the timing breakdown, the monomial-form evaluation is taking 81ms total compared to just 46ms for the DAG-form path, and critically, the monomial kernels are running per-trace without any batching. This is a clear optimization target — if I can batch the monomial evaluation kernels the way the DAG path is already batched, I could significantly reduce the overall evaluation time.\n\nLooking at the kernel breakdown, the precompute and evaluation kernels are scattered across multiple per-trace launches. The savings from batching would come from eliminating launch overhead (roughly 13ms from 2600 launches), reducing CPU-side FFI call overhead (5-13ms), and potentially improving kernel efficiency through better SM utilization on these smaller kernels (5-15ms). That adds up to roughly 23-41ms in total savings, which would bring MLE Rounds down from 170ms to around 130-147ms.\n\nThat improvement is meaningful but not transformative for the overall STARK performance. Let me reconsider whether there's a higher-impact optimization I'm missing, particularly around the precompute_logup_combinations kernel which seems to be a significant contributor.\n\nThe precompute_logup_combinations kernels run about 791 times total across all traces and rounds. The challenge here is that while the monomial coefficients from the AIR's interaction could theoretically be precomputed once, the kernel's output depends on the current round's evaluation point, which changes each round. So there's no obvious way to amortize this across rounds unless