d come from batching creating larger kernels that utilize the GPU more efficiently, since these tiny kernels are running with very low occupancy.\n\nBut looking at the bigger picture, the zerocheck and logup operations are already the heavy hitters at 16-18ms each, so occupancy gains would be incremental. Let me shift focus to the trace commit path instead — specifically the RS code matrix computation, which is taking around 57ms for one segment and likely another 30ms for the next, totaling roughly 87ms out of the 255ms trace commit time.\n\nThe RS code matrix involves transforming the stacked trace columns into polynomial coefficients via NTT, then evaluating those polynomials at extended domain points. The batch NTT kernel shows up as 32ms across 17 instances in the profiler, and combined with bit reversal at 33ms, that's about 65ms of the 87ms total. The remaining time is probably the polynomial evaluation step.\n\nThe batch NTT kernel is already highly optimized on the GPU, so there's limited room for improvement there.\n\nActually, if the traces are already in evaluation form—which they typically are in this system—the RS code matrix might just need to evaluate at additional points rather than performing a full NTT. I'm not certain about the implementation details though.\n\nLet me reconsider the WHIR kernels to see if there's per-trace overhead I'm missing. Looking at the profiler data for WHIR at APC 300, I see the algebraic batch traces kernel taking 18ms across 6 instances, the grind kernel at 18ms across 191 instances, and the fold coefficients and moments kernel at 3ms across 80 instances.\n\nThe grind kernel instances are actually repeated PoW attempts, not per-trace overhead. The fold and sumcheck kernels are per-round operations within WHIR's protocol, so there's no significant per-trace overhead to batch there. The Stacked Reduction phase already processes traces in a batched fashion, and WHIR doesn't have remaining per-trace overhead either.\n\nSo I'm going to commit to batching the monomial-form MLE precompute and evaluation kernels.\n\nBut before I finalize, let me check if there are cross-phase optimizations—like reducing CUDA binary size to improve instruction cache pressure across all phases. That could help both the APC 0 regression and APC 300 scaling, though the regression might stem from something else entirely. Looking at the scaling math: I need APC 300 below 1327ms to hit the 2.0x target, which means I need to shave off another 106ms beyond what monomial batching gives me.\n\nIf I can find multiple small wins—batching the monomial path for 40ms, optimizing trace commit for 30ms, improving round 0 load balancing for 30ms, and tweaking GKR for 20ms—those could stack to hit the target. The key is picking the optimization with the best impact-to-complexity ratio, and batching the per-monomial path looks like the strongest candidate.\n\nThe approach is proven from earlier work, the overhead is clear and measurable, and it won't ripple into other phases. If MLE Rounds drops from 170ms to 130-140ms, that gets STARK excluding trace down to around 1393-1403ms, which is meaningful progress toward the 2.0x goal.\n\nBut wait—the previous batching attempts within MLE Rounds only netted 23ms at best, and the kernel batching results were too noisy to read. Maybe I should look elsewhere. What if I focus on the evaluation kernels themselves—zerocheck_batch_mle and logup_batch_mle—which are already batched but still take 46ms combined? Optimizing those kernels directly could be more impactful than adding another batching layer.\n\nActually, I'm realizing something else: the MLE round currently does two separate passes over the traces—one for DAG-form evaluation and one for monomial-form evaluation. What if I could merge both into a single batched kernel instead of running them sequentially? That would eliminate the overhead of two separate launches and potentially give a bigger speedup than the monomial batching alone.\n\nThough now that I think about it, the DAG and monomial paths don't actually process the same traces—different traces use different evaluation methods, so they're not redundant.\n\nAlright, I'm going to commit to batching the monomial kernels. Let me dig into the actual code and work out the implementation details.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":714,"cache_read_input_tokens":105194,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":714},"output_tokens":38,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"794cedb4-74b2-4dd2-b512-5aabd1b44ebc","uuid":"99a24a29-020b-4df1-86f5-d40ca1edfbf9"}
