l launch optimization for small AIRs by grouping multiple small AIRs into fewer kernel calls. This would reduce allocation overhead and eliminate unnecessary device-to-host synchronization between small AIR processing steps.\n\nReorganizing the host loop...\n\nThe issue is that each small AIR needs its own output buffer since we can't reuse the same one, but the memory overhead is negligible since each AIR's output is just a few field elements. I'm realizing that `to_host_on_current_stream()` probably still synchronizes the current stream to ensure the host memory is ready for CPU processing, even though it's supposed to be async.\n\nThe real optimization would be to avoid syncing between kernel launches and let the GPU pipeline handle the ordering naturally. Right now we're syncing after every single AIR—allocate buffer, launch kernel, sync stream, copy data, process on CPU, free buffer—which drains the pipeline each time. If we instead batch all the kernel launches first without syncing, then sync once, then process all the copies and CPU work, we'd reduce the sync overhead from N_small down to just one sync operation.\n\nThe math is compelling: currently we're paying ~250 syncs × 50μs each = 25ms just in synchronization overhead. With batching, that drops to a single 50μs sync. The memory cost of holding separate buffers for each AIR is negligible at ~500KB, and the mutex contention for allocations is actually the same either way—the difference is we're now interleaving allocations with non-blocking kernel launches instead of with blocking syncs, which should be faster overall.\n\nLooking at the actual numbers across both segments, we're eliminating roughly 500 stream syncs per thread at 50μs each, saving around 25-35ms when accounting for thread overlap. Combined with the load balancing improvements from interleaved assignment, I'm expecting a total speedup of 40-60ms at APC 300, which would bring Round 0 down from 301ms to roughly 241-261ms and STARK trace excl