{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XvaSqFLGkdfDXvYoeCdD7D","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Cm3wA7Vd1RJH2fNUNu51aP","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-13-1930-round0-interleaved-work-balance/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Round 0 Interleaved Work Balance\n\n## Goal\n\nReduce Round 0 wall time at APC 300 by replacing contiguous work chunking with interleaved (round-robin) thread assignment. Currently, the sorted-then-chunked approach puts all large AIRs on thread 0, creating severe load imbalance (thread 0: ~200ms, thread 7: ~5ms). Interleaving distributes large/small AIRs evenly, reducing the critical thread's time and improving CPU-GPU overlap.\n\nTarget: Round 0 from 301ms to ~255-270ms at APC 300 (30-45ms improvement). STARK excl trace from 1433ms to ~1388-1403ms.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\nThe Round 0 flow in `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` (lines 899-966):\n\n1. **Phase 1 (lines 899-926):** Build `work_items: Vec<Round0AirWorkItem>` from all present AIRs. Pure CPU metadata collection.\n\n2. **Sort (line 929):** `work_items.sort_by(|a, b| b.common_main.height().cmp(&a.common_main.height()))` — sorts by descending trace height.\n\n3. **Threading decision (lines 939-943):** If `work_items.len() >= 100`, use `NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS` (8) threads; else 1 thread.\n\n4. **Chunk and dispatch (lines 950-966):**\n   ```rust\n   let chunk_size = work_items.len().div_ceil(num_threads);\n   work_items.chunks(chunk_size).map(|chunk| {\n       s.spawn(move || chunk.iter().map(|w| process_air_round0(w)).collect())\n   })\n   ```\n   With 310 AIRs and 8 threads: chunk_size = 39. Thread 0 gets items 0-38 (largest), thread 7 gets items 273-309 (smallest).\n\n5. **Per-AIR processing** in `process_air_round0` (lines 140-272): For each AIR:\n   - H2D copy: main trace pointers (~16-48 bytes)\n   - GPU kernel 1: zerocheck constraint evaluation\n   - D2H sync + CPU post-process: transpose + iDFT → polynomial\n   - GPU kernel 2: logup interaction evaluation (includes DAG construction on CPU)\n   - D2H sync + CPU post-process: transpose + iDFT → polynomial pair\n\n**The bottleneck:** Thread 0 processes all ~39 largest AIRs sequentially. Each large AIR has a GPU kernel that may take 5-16ms (saturating the GPU), plus ~1ms of CPU post-processing. Thread 0's total time: ~(200ms kernel + 39ms CPU) ≈ 240ms, which after accounting for multi-stream overlap with other threads gives the measured ~150ms per segment.\n\nWith interleaving, thread 0 would get AIRs {0, 8, 16, 24, ...} — only ~5 large AIRs instead of 39. Its kernel time drops to ~30-40ms, plus small AIRs fill the rest. The critical thread's time drops, and CPU post-processing is evenly distributed.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Replace contiguous chunking with interleaved assignment\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, lines 950-960\n\n**Current code:**\n```rust\nlet chunk_size = work_items.len().div_ceil(num_threads);\nstd::thread::scope(|s| {\n    let handles: Vec<_> = work_items\n        .chunks(chunk_size)\n        .map(|chunk| {\n            s.spawn(move || -> Result<Vec<Round0AirResult>, LogupZerocheckError> {\n                chunk.iter().map(|w| process_air_round0(w)).collect()\n            })\n        })\n        .collect();\n```\n\n**New code:** Replace `chunks(chunk_size)` with an interleaving pattern. Build per-thread work item slices using stride-based indexing:\n\n```rust\nstd::thread::scope(|s| {\n    let handles: Vec<_> = (0..num_threads)\n        .map(|thread_id| {\n            // Collect references to work items for this thread using round-robin\n            let thread_items: Vec<&Round0AirWorkItem<HS>> = work_items\n                .iter()\n                .skip(thread_id)\n                .step_by(num_threads)\n                .collect();\n            s.spawn(move || -> Result<Vec<Round0AirResult>, LogupZerocheckError> {\n                thread_items.iter().map(|w| process_air_round0(w)).collect()\n            })\n        })\n        .collect();\n```\n\n**Why:** Distributes the sorted work items (large AIRs first) evenly across threads. Thread 0 gets items {0, 8, 16, ...}, thread 1 gets {1, 9, 17, ...}. Each thread gets ~5 large AIRs + ~34 small AIRs instead of thread 0 getting 39 large and thread 7 getting 39 tiny.\n\n### Change 2: Fix result ordering after interleaving\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, result collection (lines 960-965)\n\nThe current result collection flattens thread results sequentially:\n```rust\nlet mut all_results = Vec::with_capacity(work_items.len());\nfor handle in handles {\n    all_results.extend(handle.join().unwrap()?);\n}\n```\n\nWith interleaving, results come back in interleaved order (thread 0's items, then thread 1's, ...). Each `Round0AirResult` already carries `trace_idx`, so the Phase 3 scatter (lines 968-980) uses `result.trace_idx` to place results correctly. **No change needed** — the scatter is index-based, not position-based.\n\n### Change 3: Verify sort order is preserved for interleaving benefit\n\nThe sort at line 929 (descending height) is essential for interleaving to work correctly. The round-robin pattern distributes consecutive sorted positions across threads, so height-descending sort ensures large AIRs spread evenly. **No change needed** — the sort is already correct.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness:** Every AIR is processed exactly once. The `trace_idx` field in `Round0AirResult` ensures results map to the correct output positions regardless of processing order.\n\n2. **No GPU memory regression:** Each thread still processes the same total number of AIRs (~39). The per-thread memory peak depends on the LARGEST AIR in that thread's set. With interleaving, the largest AIR (position 0) is still on thread 0, so thread 0's peak memory is unchanged. Other threads now have slightly larger peaks (they get some large AIRs) but the total concurrent memory is bounded by `memory_limit_bytes / NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS` per thread (unchanged).\n\n3. **APC 0 no-regression:** At APC 0, `work_items.len() < 100` so single-threaded path is taken. The interleaving change is in the multi-threaded branch only.\n\n4. **Thread safety:** `process_air_round0` is already thread-safe (uses `cudaStreamPerThread`, no shared mutable state). Interleaving doesn't change the parallelism model.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\nRun the full benchmark suite for APC {0, 100, 300}:\n```bash\ncd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing\n/path/to/powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics /tmp/after_apc300.json --recursion\n/path/to/powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc100.cbor --input 0 --metrics /tmp/after_apc100.json --recursion\n/path/to/powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics /tmp/after_apc000.json --recursion\n```\n\nThen analyze with `spec.py`. Key metrics:\n- Round 0 at APC 300: expect 255-270ms (from 301ms)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300: expect 1388-1403ms (from 1433ms)\n- APC 0: no regression expected (single-threaded path)\n\nRun APC 300 3x to confirm stability of Round 0 improvement.\n\nProfile with nsys to verify improved GPU utilization:\n```bash\nnsys profile --trace cuda,nvtx --sample none -- /path/to/binary prove --artifact apc300.cbor ...\n```\nCompare CUDA stream activity: look for more uniform kernel distribution across streams.\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- Round 0 at APC 300 improves by less than 15ms → revert\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300 regresses or improves by less than 15ms → revert\n- APC 0 STARK excl trace regresses by more than 20ms → revert\n- Any APC configuration fails to prove+verify → revert\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":431,"cache_read_input_tokens":188178,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":431},"output_tokens":68,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"794cedb4-74b2-4dd2-b512-5aabd1b44ebc","uuid":"6e44b339-8b1f-4cd9-991f-5590b8624e5b"}
