{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01H5ricXxXVv8hxyYae4AiEj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Round 0 Interleaved Work Balance\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReduce Round 0 wall time at APC 300 by replacing contiguous work chunking with greedy longest-processing-time-first (LPT) thread assignment. Currently, the sorted-then-chunked approach puts all large AIRs on thread 0, creating load imbalance. LPT distributes work so that each thread's total estimated cost is approximately equal, improving CPU-GPU overlap during post-processing phases.\n6\t\n7\tThe performance hypothesis: with contiguous chunking, thread 0 (holding all large AIRs) is the critical path because it accumulates both the largest GPU kernel waits AND the most CPU post-processing. Even though GPU SM saturation limits kernel concurrency (large kernels monopolize SMs regardless of which thread holds them), the CPU post-processing between kernels on the critical thread adds serial overhead that does not overlap with other threads' GPU work. By distributing large AIRs across threads, each thread's CPU post-processing interleaves with other threads' GPU kernel execution, reducing total wall time.\n8\t\n9\t**Important caveat:** Prior Round 0 optimizations (pipeline-round0-kernel-launches, parallelize-round0-cpu-postprocess) showed that the gap between GPU kernel time and total Round 0 time is \"primarily D2H copy latency and kernel launch overhead.\" The load balancing change addresses a different aspect — it reduces the critical thread's accumulated CPU time between GPU kernel launches, not the per-AIR overhead. The expected improvement is conservative: 15-30ms.\n10\t\n11\tTarget: Round 0 from ~295ms to ~265-280ms at APC 300 (15-30ms improvement). STARK excl trace from ~1433ms to ~1403-1418ms.\n12\t\n13\t## Current Code Path\n14\t\n15\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n16\t\n17\tThe Round 0 flow in `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` (lines 899-966):\n18\t\n19\t1. **Phase 1 (lines 899-926):** Build `work_items: Vec<Round0AirWorkItem>` from all present AIRs. Pure CPU metadata collection.\n20\t\n21\t2. **Sort (line 929):** `work_items.sort_by(|a, b| b.common_main.height().cmp(&a.common_main.height()))` — sorts by descending trace height.\n22\t\n23\t3. **Threading decision (lines 939-943):** If `work_items.len() >= 100`, use `NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS` (8) threads; else 1 thread.\n24\t\n25\t4. **Chunk and dispatch (lines 950-966):**\n26\t   ```rust\n27\t   let chunk_size = work_items.len().div_ceil(num_threads);\n28\t   work_items.chunks(chunk_size).map(|chunk| {\n29\t       s.spawn(move || chunk.iter().map(|w| process_air_round0(w)).collect())\n30\t   })\n31\t   ```\n32\t   With 310 AIRs and 8 threads: chunk_size = 39. Thread 0 gets items 0-38 (largest), thread 7 gets items 273-309 (smallest).\n33\t\n34\t5. **Per-AIR processing** in `process_air_round0` (lines 140-272): For each AIR:\n35\t   - H2D copy: main trace pointers (~16-48 bytes)\n36\t   - GPU kernel 1: zerocheck constraint evaluation\n37\t   - D2H sync + CPU post-process: transpose + iDFT → polynomial\n38\t   - GPU kernel 2: logup interaction evaluation (includes DAG construction on CPU)\n39\t   - D2H sync + CPU post-process: transpose + iDFT → polynomial pair\n40\t\n41\t## Changes\n42\t\n43\t### Change 0 (diagnostic): Add per-thread timing instrumentation\n44\t\n45\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, inside the thread spawn\n46\t\n47\tBefore implementing the LPT assignment, add `Instant::now()` / `elapsed()` measurements around each thread's work loop. Log per-thread completion times at debug level. This validates the load imbalance hypothesis — confirming whether thread 0 actually finishes much later than thread 7.\n48\t\n49\t```rust\n50\ts.spawn(move || -> Result<Vec<Round0AirResult>, LogupZerocheckError> {\n51\t    let t0 = std::time::Instant::now();\n52\t    let results: Vec<_> = chunk.iter().map(|w| process_air_round0(w)).collect::<Result<_, _>>()?;\n53\t    tracing::debug!(thread_id, elapsed_ms = t0.elapsed().as_millis(), num_airs = chunk.len(), \"round0 thread done\");\n54\t    Ok(results)\n55\t})\n56\t```\n57\t\n58\tIf all threads finish within ~10ms of each other, abandon the LPT approach (the bottleneck is GPU SM saturation, not thread imbalance). If thread 0 is >50ms slower than the median, proceed with Change 1.\n59\t\n60\t### Change 1: Replace contiguous chunking with greedy LPT assignment\n61\t\n62\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, lines 950-960\n63\t\n64\tUse a greedy longest-processing-time-first (LPT) algorithm: iterate sorted work items (largest first) and assign each to the thread with the lowest current total estimated cost. Use `common_main.height()` as the cost proxy (height correlates with kernel runtime).\n65\t\n66\t```rust\n67\t// Greedy LPT assignment: assign each work item to the least-loaded thread\n68\tlet mut thread_loads = vec![0u64; num_threads];\n69\tlet mut thread_items: Vec<Vec<&Round0AirWorkItem<HS>>> =\n70\t    (0..num_threads).map(|_| Vec::new()).collect();\n71\tfor item in work_items.iter() {\n72\t    // Find thread with minimum load\n73\t    let min_thread = thread_loads\n74\t        .iter()\n75\t        .enumerate()\n76\t        .min_by_key(|(_, load)| *load)\n77\t        .unwrap()\n78\t        .0;\n79\t    thread_items[min_thread].push(item);\n80\t    thread_loads[min_thread] += item.common_main.height() as u64;\n81\t}\n82\t\n83\tstd::thread::scope(|s| {\n84\t    let handles: Vec<_> = thread_items\n85\t        .into_iter()\n86\t        .enumerate()\n87\t        .map(|(thread_id, items)| {\n88\t            s.spawn(move || -> Result<Vec<Round0AirResult>, LogupZerocheckError> {\n89\t                let t0 = std::time::Instant::now();\n90\t                let results: Vec<_> = items.iter().map(|w| process_air_round0(w)).collect::<Result<_, _>>()?;\n91\t                tracing::debug!(thread_id, elapsed_ms = t0.elapsed().as_millis(), num_airs = items.len(), \"round0 thread done\");\n92\t                Ok(results)\n93\t            })\n94\t        })\n95\t        .collect();\n96\t```\n97\t\n98\t**Why LPT over round-robin:** Round-robin on a sorted list is a reasonable heuristic but does not account for the wide variance in AIR heights. For example, if one AIR has height 2^20 (1M) and the next has 2^19 (512K), round-robin assigns them to threads 0 and 1. But the 2^20 AIR has roughly 2x the work. LPT accounts for this by assigning the 2^19 AIR to a different, less-loaded thread.\n99\t\n100\t**Why `height` as cost proxy:** The constraint evaluation and interaction evaluation kernel runtime scales approximately linearly with trace height (for the coset-parallel kernel, the work is `height × num_cosets × constraint_complexity`). Height is the dominant factor and is available without additional computation.\n101\t\n102\t### Change 2: Concurrent GPU memory analysis\n103\t\n104\t**File:** Same as Change 1.\n105\t\n106\tWith LPT, each thread now holds some large AIRs. The per-thread temporary allocation budget is `memory_limit_bytes / NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS` (line 900), which is unchanged. The max allocation per AIR within a thread is bounded by this budget, enforced by `evaluate_round0_constraints_gpu` and `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu`. Since each AIR allocates and frees its temporaries before the next AIR starts (sequential within each thread), the per-thread peak memory does not increase with interleaving.\n107\t\n108\tHowever, with contiguous chunking, thread 0 finishes large AIR allocations before thread 7 starts its small ones. With LPT, all threads allocate large-AIR temporaries concurrently. The worst case is `8 × (memory_limit_bytes / 8) = memory_limit_bytes`, which is the same total. No change needed, but monitor GPU memory usage in measurements.\n109\t\n110\t## Invariants\n111\t\n112\t1. **Correctness:** Every AIR is processed exactly once. The `trace_idx` field in `Round0AirResult` ensures results map to the correct output positions regardless of processing order.\n113\t\n114\t2. **Per-thread memory budget:** Unchanged at `memory_limit_bytes / NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS`.\n115\t\n116\t3. **APC 0 no-regression:** At APC 0, `work_items.len() < 100` so single-threaded path is taken. LPT change is in the multi-threaded branch only.\n117\t\n118\t4. **Thread safety:** `process_air_round0` is already thread-safe (uses `cudaStreamPerThread`, no shared mutable state). LPT doesn't change the parallelism model.\n119\t\n120\t## Measurement Plan\n121\t\n122\t### Step 1: Diagnostic instrumentation run\n123\tBuild with per-thread timing (Change 0). Run APC 300 once. Check log output for per-thread elapsed times. If thread imbalance is <20ms, stop and report — the approach cannot achieve meaningful improvement.\n124\t\n125\t### Step 2: LPT implementation + benchmark\n126\tIf instrumentation confirms >30ms thread imbalance, implement Change 1. Run the full benchmark suite for APC {0, 100, 300}:\n127\t```bash\n128\tcd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing\n129\t/path/to/powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics /tmp/after_apc300.json --recursion\n130\t/path/to/powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc100.cbor --input 0 --metrics /tmp/after_apc100.json --recursion\n131\t/path/to/powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics /tmp/after_apc000.json --recursion\n132\t```\n133\t\n134\tAnalyze with `spec.py`. Run APC 300 3x to confirm stability.\n135\t\n136\t### Step 3: Compare per-thread timing before/after\n137\tCompare the per-thread elapsed times from Change 0's instrumentation with and without LPT. Verify that the slowest thread's time decreased.\n138\t\n139\t## Rollback Criteria\n140\t\n141\t- Diagnostic instrumentation shows thread imbalance <20ms → do not proceed with LPT\n142\t- Round 0 at APC 300 improves by less than 15ms → revert\n143\t- STARK excl trace at APC 300 regresses or improves by less than 15ms → revert\n144\t- APC 0 STARK excl trace regresses by more than 20ms → revert\n145\t- Any APC configuration fails to prove+verify → revert\n146\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Hyo7cDXu8TGL7CUDxcu2rg","session_id":"794cedb4-74b2-4dd2-b512-5aabd1b44ebc","uuid":"c7ed75cb-07f4-4790-8476-bbc46596dba5","timestamp":"2026-04-13T18:15:54.473Z"}
