ch is reasonable as a first approximation since kernel runtime scales with height, but the diagnostic instrumentation in Change 0 would reveal whether the imbalance matches height-based predictions.\n\n## Improvements I would consider\n\n### A. Add per-AIR timing within Change 0, not just per-thread totals\n\nThe diagnostic instrumentation logs per-thread completion time but not per-AIR breakdown. Adding per-AIR timing (at least for the top 10 largest AIRs) would directly validate whether `height` is a good cost proxy and whether CPU post-processing or GPU kernel time dominates per-AIR cost. This is cheap to add and dramatically increases the diagnostic value.\n\n### B. Consider measuring the actual CPU post-processing time separately from GPU wait time\n\nWithin each AIR, the `to_host_on_current_stream` call includes both D2H copy time and stream sync (waiting for the kernel). The CPU work (transpose + iDFT + polynomial construction) follows. Instrumenting these separately would reveal whether the load imbalance is in GPU kernel wait time (where LPT helps via better SM sharing) or in CPU post-processing (where LPT helps via overlap). The answer determines whether the gain is real or whether SM saturation makes the GPU component unresponsive to rebalancing.\n\n### C. Consider a simpler random shuffle instead of LPT\n\nIf the dominant effect is just avoiding the pathological case where all large AIRs land on one thread, a random shuffle of `work_items` before chunking would achieve similar balance with zero algorithmic complexity. LPT is more principled but adds code. If the diagnostic shows modest imbalance (20-30ms), a shuffle might capture most of the gain with less code.\n\n## Assessment\n\nThere is no remaining design-level correctness blocker. The plan preserves all invariants (trace_idx-based result ordering, per-thread memory budget, APC 0 single-threaded path, thread safety). The code changes are localized to lines 950-960 of `mod.rs`.\n\nThe strongest part of this plan is Change 0: the diagnostic instrumentation with a clear stop/go gate. This directly addresses the primary concern from the prior review -- the lack of evidence for the load imbalance hypothesis. If the hypothesis is wrong (thread imbalance < 20ms), the plan correctly avoids wasting effort on LPT.\n\nThe plan is prototype-ready. The expected gain (15-30ms) is at the low end of what constitutes a meaningful optimization on a ~295ms span, and the actual gain may be closer to 10-15ms given the prior evidence that CPU post-processing overhead is limited. However, with the diagnostic gate, the worst case is spending one instrumentation run to learn something valuable about the system, which is a reasonable cost. The LPT implementation itself is small and low-risk."}],"totalDurationMs":105705,"totalTokens":31200,"totalToolUseCount":17,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":838,"cache_read_input_tokens":28311,"output_tokens":2050,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":838},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2050,"cache_read_input_tokens":28311,"cache_creation_input_tokens":838,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":838,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":11,"searchCount":6,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
